# 5dive-release-hold v1
#
# THE FLEET BRAKE (DIVE-4223). Every box's `5dive self-update` re-fetches
# install.sh from `main` on every run, so this file — read from `main`, never
# from the tag being installed — is a live fleet control plane.
#
# TO ARM A HOLD: add ONE line naming the release tag to hold back, then commit
# and push to main. It takes effect on each box at its next self-update (the
# fleet's is 04:00Z); the raw CDN adds a few minutes.
#
#     v0.15.34   red nightly smoke 2026-09-10, onboarding wizard dead — <who>
#
# TO REVERT: delete the line and push. One commit each way.
#
# LINE SHAPE, exactly: a bare vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH tag at column 1, optionally
# followed by whitespace and a human reason. Anything else — a leading space, a
# `#`, a `v0.15` — is NOT a hold and will be ignored in silence. Under incident
# pressure, copy the line above.
#
# WHAT A HOLD DOES: install.sh's resolve_gh_tag() walks the version-sorted tag
# list DOWN from the newest and skips every tag listed here, so boxes land on the
# newest release that is NOT held. `5dive update --check` reads the same file, so
# it reports the same version the installer would actually deliver.
#
# WHAT A HOLD DOES NOT DO: it holds boxes BACK, it does not pull them back. A box
# that already installed the held tag stays on it — DIVE-2243's monotonicity
# guard refuses the downgrade. This bounds how many boxes reach a bad release; it
# does not undo the ones that did.
#
# DO NOT DELETE THIS FILE, and do not empty it. Its absence is not "no hold" —
# both readers treat an unfetchable or bannerless body as UNKNOWN and fail
# CLOSED, which stops every upgrade on the fleet until it is restored.
#
# Held tags below this line. None currently.
